 "questions": [
  {
   "id": 535574,
   "is_answer_order_important": true,
   "stimulus_html": "",
   "stem_html": "<p><img src=\"exercises/media/edcf98979896aaab.jpg\" alt=\"The illustration shows a mast cell producing histamine. Histamine receptors are shown on vessel cells and nerve cells with anti-histamine bound to the histamine receptors.\"></p>\n\n<p>An allergy is caused by the immune system reacting to a foreign protein to produce IgE molecules that recognize the protein. These IgE molecules become associated with mast cells that respond to future exposures to the protein by releasing histamines into the body. The diagram shows this release and also shows how a drug called an antihistamine can help an allergy sufferer reduce their allergy symptoms. Which of the following statements explains how an antihistamine helps restore homeostasis during an allergic reaction?</p>\n",
   "answers": [
    {
     "id": 1048592,
     "content_html": "Antihistamines prevent mast cells from becoming associated with IgE molecules that recognize the foreign protein allergen."
    },
    {
     "id": 1048593,
     "content_html": "Antihistamines prevent mast cells from releasing histamines and causing the unpleasant allergy symptoms."
    },
    {
     "id": 1048594,
     "content_html": "Antihistamines prevent histamines that have been released by mast cells from stimulating the itching and swelling of body tissues."
    },
    {
     "id": 1048595,
     "content_html": "Antihistamines prevent mast cells from producing histamines, which halts their effect on the body."
    }
   ],
